   "about": "GenAI engineer specializing in production multi-agent orchestration, RAG systems, and agentic automation. Seven years building enterprise software at a Fortune 25 retailer, with the past 18 months focused on production GenAI systems, including a multi-agent support automation platform and a RAG service for grounded instructional content.\n\nMy work sits at the intersection of infrastructure, intelligence, and human workflows. I architect systems that make ambiguous spaces tractable, so teams can ship with confidence and scale solutions that last. I've delivered mission-critical integration infrastructure through a multi-year legacy modernization, taken over incomplete third-party work in legally sensitive domains and brought it to production, and solo-owned a production Salesforce org through significant business scaling.\n\nTechnical foundation: GCP-native architectural depth (ADK, Vertex AI, Cloud Run, BigQuery), production agent orchestration, RAG engineering with observability-first reliability, and Python-centered full-stack delivery. I specialize in stitching together fractured ecosystems and making moving pieces legible, whether for architects, security reviewers, or future engineering owners.\n\nI treat AI ethics as an engineering constraint rather than an afterthought. I'm deliberate about where and how systems are allowed to make decisions, and I architect for safety, transparency, and human oversight from the start. AI is a powerful accelerant, not a replacement for human judgment, and my work finds the margin where it reduces overhead and scales capability without compromising accountability.\n\nOutside enterprise engineering, I'm a co-founder of Pneuma Games, an indie game studio with a distributed international team of artists, sound engineers, writers, and composers, currently developing a commercial title in partnership with a major legacy IP holder. I'm also a co-founder of Vital Foundry, a GenAI workflow automation business serving small-to-mid-size clients, where we apply enterprise-grade production patterns to businesses that typically can't afford that level of infrastructure.",
